<!-- 4-LAYER DETECTION ENGINE -->
<section class="vx-section" aria-labelledby="engine-title">
  <div class="vx-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Detection Engine</div>
    <h2 id="engine-title">Four analysis layers</h2>
    <p>Alongside vulnerability database lookups, vexes reads package code, dependency graphs, version history, and registry metadata. Each layer produces signals, not verdicts: they point you at packages worth reading before you trust them.</p>
    <div class="vx-features-grid vx-features-grid--pairs" role="list">
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 1 — AST Code Analysis</h3>
        <p>Parses JavaScript and Python source via acorn AST. Looks for <code>eval()</code>, <code>child_process.exec()</code>, credential-file reads, obfuscated code, WebAssembly payloads, DNS exfiltration, and prototype chain escapes. Plenty of legitimate packages use these, so a hit means read the code.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 2 — Dependency Graph</h3>
        <p>Profiles newly added dependencies. Flags packages under 7 days old, names a short edit distance from popular ones, Unicode homoglyphs, and circular staging by the same author.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 3 — Behavioral Fingerprinting</h3>
        <p>Diffs capability profiles between versions. Flags a utility library that gains network or process-execution capability in a new release. Refactors do this too, so it is a prompt to read the diff.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 4 — Registry Metadata</h3>
        <p>Analyzes publish history, maintainer account changes, rapid-fire releases, dormant package reactivation, and Sigstore provenance attestations.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

